course: dictionary
lesson: 3320
title: Evacuation: leaving danger safely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An evacuation is when people leave a dangerous place quickly and in an organised way. The context helps: «When there is a wildfire evacuation order, leave early with your family» shows that people leave because of danger, and they leave together, following a plan.
explain: The clues in «never go back for toys; roads can become blocked» show why this matters: you do not stop for things, because every minute counts. Ask yourself what would fit here. Leaving a burning building or a town before a flood fits; playing a game does not.
explain: The word also has a meaning from history. In wartime, evacuation was moving children from cities to the countryside to keep them safe. You may see the phrases «evacuation drill», «evacuation route» and «evacuation centre» at school or in the news.
fact: Evacuation is a noun: an evacuation, evacuations.
fact: «Evacuation» means leaving a dangerous place quickly and in an organised way.
fact: An evacuation order is an official instruction to leave an area.
fact: In a fire drill, you stop, line up quietly and walk, not run, to the assembly point.
fact: In history, evacuation meant moving children from cities to the countryside during wartime, as British children were in World War II.
exercise: «When there is a wildfire evacuation order, leave early with your family.» What does evacuation mean here? | Fighting the fire with buckets | Buying new toys for the family | Staying home to watch the fire | *Leaving a dangerous place quickly and in an organised way
exercise: In a fire drill, what should you do? | *Stop, line up quietly and walk to the assembly point | Hide under your desk until it is over | Go back inside for your bag | Run to get your favourite things
exercise: In history, what was evacuation during wartime? | Sending children to work in factories | Building new roads in the cities | Teaching children to swim | *Moving children from cities to the countryside to keep them safe
test: Which phrase names the path people take to leave safely? | Evacuation centre | Evacuation drill | *Evacuation route | Evacuation order
test: Why should you never go back for things during an evacuation? | *Because roads can become blocked and every minute counts | Because it is more fun to stay | Because things are always safer inside | Because you can carry everything at once
exam: «Before a hurricane or cyclone, follow evacuation orders.» What should you do when you hear an evacuation order? | Ignore it if the sky looks clear | *Leave the area as instructed, without going back for things | Wait at home to see what happens | Go outside to check the wind
